is a different tree entirely. `verdict.py:50`\n35\t   records \"the loop measured at `HEAD` instead\" as the original two-reader\n36\t   disagreement, `governing_rev`'s and `governing_identity`'s docstrings both\n37\t   say \"`rev` defaults to the branch tip\", and `format_branch_trailer`\n38\t   (`:738-740`) forbids the word by name — \"`branch` must therefore be the\n39\t   lane's BRANCH NAME and not `HEAD`: the peel verifies a refresh commit\n40\t   against the branch it names, and `HEAD` matches no refresh subject.\"\n41\t   Restate the clause as the branch tip or an explicit revision, and say that\n42\t   the branch argument is a branch NAME, for the reason the module gives.\n43\t2. `LLR-207.detail`, the `governing_rev` clause. It reads \"walks across commits\n44\t   whose non-record identity equals their first parent's UNTIL IT CAN PEEL a\n45\t   verified refresh\". That is not the termination condition. A peel does not\n46\t   end the walk — it re-seats `rev` and `continue`s (`verdict.py:465-468`) —\n47\t   and the walk ends at the first commit whose identity differs from its\n48\t   parent's (`:473`), or at the absent-parent / `_MAX_GOVERNING_WALK` bounds\n49\t   (`:470`, `:398`). A branch with no refresh under it therefore still walks,\n50\t   which is the whole OI-76 fix; a builder implementing the sentence literally\n51\t   returns the tip and reintroduces it. `IF-175` already states the mechanism\n52\t   correctly — \"to reach a refresh it would otherwise hide\" — so borrow that\n53\t   framing: the refresh is the walk's PURPOSE, not its terminus.\n54\t3. The multi-log ambiguity rule has no detector. `LLR-207.detail` states \"a\n55\t   joined key whose logs declare more than one review phase yields no round\"\n56\t   and `verdict.py:608` implements it (`len(ph) == 1`). No fixture writes TWO\n57\t   session logs at one `(train, ordinal)`. Mutation driven and reverted:\n58\t   relaxing that guard to last-wins (`sorted(ph)[-1] ... if ph`) left\n59\t   `134 